#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097496} :activity/id #uuid "23266c50-a061-5ecc-b648-05f71a1b5ec6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049619 :topic/id #uuid "0536fa23-cd17-50b6-b180-7b50962fb1ee" :topic/math-academy-id 1153} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097496 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097924 :tutorial/content "An **exponential function** is a function where a variable is contained within an exponent. For example, the function below is an exponential function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = 3^x\n$$\n\nJust like any other function, we can evaluate it for some particular value of $x,$ say at $x={\\color{blue}{4}},$ by substituting this value of $x$ into the function:\n\n$$\nf(4) = 3^{\\color{blue}{4}} = 81\n$$\n\nAnother example of an exponential function is $g(x)$ below:\n\n$$\ng(x) = 16\\cdot \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^x\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this function at $x={\\color{blue}{4}}$ using the power of quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(4) & =16\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4} \\\\  & =16\\cdot \\frac{{1}^{4}}{{2}^{4}} \\\\  & =16\\cdot \\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExponential functions are of huge practical importance in science, engineering, economics, and many other disciplines. Let's practice evaluating them." :tutorial/id #uuid "155b9a63-2c55-5f26-a41f-b777e7afa911" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8843 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "28a95194-58c2-5aab-b5ec-1edc7f9c4953" :step/math-academy-id 166109 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097497}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097497 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096657 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096390 :question/id #uuid "d03736b3-a7d8-52b3-894d-4ce2d04ee31c" :question/math-academy-id "e-14257"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9283940a-cad8-51b5-b8c2-dc42ad8de264" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048683}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488193}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Exponential Function"} :step/id #uuid "e368b5da-f79a-5928-9c93-43f4a0dee7a5" :step/math-academy-id 166110 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097498}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097498 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096658 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096391 :question/id #uuid "a83c1301-18cf-5622-8a4a-73f9d18e36b8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14260"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bab04f2-5e9a-55e7-9669-63e15938be0a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508982}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Function in a Modeling Problem"} :step/id #uuid "103957a3-310c-54ce-8227-8f216b6f4683" :step/math-academy-id 166114 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097499}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097499 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097925 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we're given the following exponential function:\n\n$$\nf(t) = C\\cdot 4^t\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that $f(3) = 128.$ Can we use this information to find the unknown constant $C?$\n\nTo solve this problem, we substitute $t=3$ and $f(t) = 128$ and solve for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}128 & =C\\cdot {4}^{3} \\\\ 128 & =C\\cdot 64 \\\\ \\frac{128}{64} & =C \\\\ 2 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $C=2.$ So our function expressed in full is\n\n$$\nf(t) = 2\\cdot 4^t.\n$$\n\nWe sometimes say that the **initial value** of the function is $2,$ because if we evaluate $f$ at $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\nf(0) = 2\\cdot 4^0 = 2\\cdot 1 = 2.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6820cdab-7e0a-5a90-adb1-880e4eb4a8de" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8846 :tutorial/title "Solving for an Unknown Constant"} :step/id #uuid "0d53b42c-c931-5e58-b073-7217b0bb9ac4" :step/math-academy-id 166118 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097500}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097500 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096659 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096392 :question/id #uuid "ca8cd4d6-8136-59bb-a033-6ba9ae8c06ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-14262"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cee2b0-6cc8-5281-8ea2-02400b2980e1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050613}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508929}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Initial Value"} :step/id #uuid "546da0c9-4c8f-57c2-9e29-200dc3ee9f2d" :step/math-academy-id 166119 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097501}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097501 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097926 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following exponential function:\n\n$$\nh(x) = 2^x\n$$\n\nCan we find the value of $x$ that gives $h(x)=64?$ Indeed, we can. To do this, we'll need to use our knowledge of exponential equations. \n\nFirst, we use the fact that $h(x)=64$ to write our equation as\n\n$$\n2^x = 64.\n$$\n\nThis is an exponential equation. Since we know that $64$ is a power of $2,$ we can solve this equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x} & =64 \\\\ {2}^{x} & ={2}^{6} \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "94964a23-690b-5cea-8e8c-5946840cca5d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8848 :tutorial/title "Solving for an Unknown Input Value"} :step/id #uuid "2934358a-1be2-5bac-84d5-feb8432e9804" :step/math-academy-id 166122 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097502}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097502 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096660 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096393 :question/id #uuid "02cbb721-a880-5600-8093-fc768ae5e993" :question/math-academy-id "e-14264"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a21f047-5100-5f8b-9448-a5c2044e4e65" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050511}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488280}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Input Value"} :step/id #uuid "499e92bc-8919-5fbf-8915-9df0c0cca3d7" :step/math-academy-id 166123}] :activity/title "Exponential Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096918}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509046} :activity/id #uuid "7c388d04-2b80-59a8-b4dd-4cabcf69a693" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049619 :topic/id #uuid "0536fa23-cd17-50b6-b180-7b50962fb1ee" :topic/math-academy-id 1153} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509006 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "363f8683-7ce6-519e-b516-88ec0639bc77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\,293$ subscribers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508471} {:answer/id #uuid "7999765f-0877-5da0-8674-68c82949589f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$11\\,185$ subscribers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508568} {:answer/id #uuid "83349184-0745-5f0a-adb8-dc217a25b73d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$3\\,645$ subscribers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508584} {:answer/id #uuid "bc607cfb-7458-58ee-9fe1-447d861318c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$32\\,805$ subscribers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508676} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f48a1a-dec7-5522-a1f4-4f63c0ce08a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$234\\,375$ subscribers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508736}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508471} :answer-field/id #uuid "595c2d53-60ff-5406-b569-8b0372ade376" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1571510a-8d3e-5007-9b28-e17c3cb5bde3" :question/math-academy-id "q-112148" :question/problem "The number of subscribers to a video channel is given by $S(t)=5\\cdot (2.4)^{t},$ where $t$ is the time in days since the channel was opened. How many subscribers will the channel have after a week? Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "To get the number of subscribers to the video channel after a week ($7$ days), we evaluate the function at $t=7.$ Substituting $t=7$ into the function $S(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(7) & =5\\cdot (2.4)^{7} \\\\  & ≈2\\,293.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, after a week, the video channel will have $2\\,293$ subscribers."} :step/id #uuid "2c28505c-84c4-5461-afbd-0a3de162478c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509038}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509038 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9063cde2-3330-509e-ae49-cc65532f4b9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508607}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508607} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6b57ab6-1b1d-515d-affc-7350d9f0ce89" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4fda74bd-4783-5b76-9154-8ddd3936c772" :question/math-academy-id "q-218594" :question/problem "If $f(x)=A\\cdot {2}^{x}$ and $f(6)=192,$ then $A=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=6$ and $f(x)=192$ into the expression for $f(x)$ and then solve for $A,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}192 & =A\\cdot {2}^{6} \\\\ \\frac{192}{{2}^{6}} & =A \\\\ \\frac{192}{64} & =A \\\\ 3 & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "d1d64943-9a98-5436-a9cf-ea3fcf7adb24"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509046 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f7d7512-00dd-53a1-a339-e816ce6f9bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$0.04$ gallons" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508412} {:answer/id #uuid "4a011cf2-f11f-556b-9630-c5475d45f2d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$0.2$ gallons" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508497} {:answer/id #uuid "9528fa7d-db24-588f-95a3-5f14223601ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1$ gallon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508611} {:answer/id #uuid "a758b6c6-f6c3-5428-a865-833457877121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$0.02$ gallons" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508640} {:answer/id #uuid "d808d943-003d-59e8-a1b2-bb5ce6ae207f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$0.4$ gallons" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508739}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508412} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bbf46a9-a271-5e94-8244-41b134f4a887" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d4c0de90-e894-5c05-811c-ac221194e0c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-112129" :question/problem "A five gallons water bottle is leaking its content. The number of gallons of water left inside the bottle after $t$ minutes is given by the formula $W(t)=5\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{t}.$ How many gallons of water will be inside the bottle after $3$ minutes?" :question/worked-solution "To get the number of gallons of water inside the bottle after $3$ minutes, we evaluate the function at $t=3.$ Substituting $t=3$ into the function $W(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & =5\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{3} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{5}^{3}}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{125} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{125} \\\\  & =0.04.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, after $3$ minutes, there will be $0.04$ gallons of water inside the bottle."} :step/id #uuid "f31a4f50-f5a8-571a-bc6d-1143b83e36a5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509006}}] :activity/title "Exponential Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508381}]}
